# 🔐 DYNAMIC PORT FORWARDING (SSH SOCKS PROXY)

---

# 🟢 BASIC LEVEL

---

## 1. Basic dynamic forwarding

```bash id="d1"
ssh -D 1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

---

### 🧠 Meaning:

| Part             | Meaning             |
| ---------------- | ------------------- |
| `-D`             | Dynamic SOCKS proxy |
| `1080`           | Local proxy port    |
| `user@SERVER_IP` | SSH server          |

---

### 🔄 Flow:

```text id="d2"
Your Apps → localhost:1080 (SOCKS)
        ↓
SSH Tunnel
        ↓
Remote Server → Internet
```

---

### ✔ Use case:

* Secure browsing through server
* Basic proxy setup

---

# 🟡 INTERMEDIATE LEVEL

---

## 2. Run in background

```bash id="i1"
ssh -f -N -D 1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

---

### Meaning:

* `-f` → run in background
* `-N` → no shell (tunnel only)

✔ Keeps proxy running silently

---

## 3. Bind to specific local interface

```bash id="i2"
ssh -D 127.0.0.1:1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

✔ Safer (only your machine can use proxy)

---

## 4. Use different proxy port

```bash id="i3"
ssh -D 8080 user@SERVER_IP
```

✔ Avoid port conflicts

---

# 🔵 ADVANCED LEVEL

---

## 5. Force specific encryption + compression (faster tunnels)

```bash id="a1"
ssh -C -D 1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

---

### Meaning:

* `-C` → compression enabled

✔ Useful on slow networks

---

## 6. Combine with strict routing (no DNS leaks)

```bash id="a2"
ssh -D 1080 -o "ProxyCommand none" user@SERVER_IP
```

✔ Prevents accidental proxy bypass

---

## 7. System-wide SOCKS routing (advanced usage)

Instead of only browser:

* configure OS proxy settings

```text id="a3"
SOCKS5 = 127.0.0.1:1080
```

✔ All supported apps use tunnel

---

## 8. Persistent tunnel (production style)

```bash id="a4"
autossh -M 0 -f -N -D 1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

✔ Auto-reconnect if connection drops
✔ Used in real environments

